          %h2#idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p
              I am not a “Hegelian”. F. W. Lawvere
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p
            %strong Aufhebung
            (sublation) is a central concept
            %sup#fnref:2
              %a{:href => "#fn:2", :rel => "footnote"} 2
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dialectic"} dialectical
            logic of the German philosopher
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"} G. W. F. Hegel
            %em tollere, elevare, conservare
            would be Latin equivalents.
            %sup#fnref:3
              %a{:href => "#fn:3", :rel => "footnote"} 3
          %p
            In his quest to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatize
            the concepts of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"} F. W. Lawvere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            proposed a mathematical rendering of the
            %em Aufhebungs
            relation within
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            more generally. It is the mathematical concept that will constitute the primary subject in the following.
          %h2#aufhebung_in_hegels_wissenschaft_der_logik
            %em Aufhebung
            in Hegel’s ‘Wissenschaft der Logik’
          %p
            Although the two volumes of
            %em
              ‘
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"}> Wissenschaft der Logik
              ’
            (1st ed. 1812-1816) can be considered as one of the main texts of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ontology"} ontology
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+philosophy"} natural philosophical
            tradition in the vein of F. Engels petrified to the doctrines of dialectical materialism.
          %p
            The ‘Wissenschaft der Logik’ has to be viewed against the background of philosophy in the early 19th century:
            %strong Kant
            had embarked on a project of ‘refoundation’, or rather demolition, of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaphysics"} metaphysics
            from an epistemological perspective and this project had been pushed further by his followers, especially Fichte in his
            = succeed "." do
              %em Wissensschaftslehre
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idealism"} idealist
            systems had been to the claims of traditional metaphysics and epistemology, they all left the traditional logic untouched and in this respect fell behind Leibniz. It is at this point where Hegel starts: he sets out to extend the critical examination of the foundations of knowledge to logic itself.
          %p
            Heavily influenced by the transcendental deductions and the chapters on dialectical paralogisms in Kant’s ‘Kritik der reinen Vernunft’, he intends to start from indeterminate, immediate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/being"} being
            and justify the autonomous development of the system of categories. Here dialectics and Aufhebung enter the picture as Hegel conceives the categories not only as not given apriorily but as actually
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} becoming
            %em logic ceases to be an inventory of categories but becomes a system of transformations of categories!
            (Had Eilenberg and MacLane in 1945 intended their terminological loans from philosophy as a kind of joke, Lawvere would 25 years later take this terminological proximity at face value.)
          %p
            Hegel parts with the traditional conception mainly in two points: the foundations of his logic coalesce with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ontology"} ontology
            into an
            %strong objective logic
            as the first part is titled (a ‘logic of things’ as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Sanders+Peirce"} C.S. Peirce
            would later put it), i.e. he rejects the subject as a possible ground for logic, and he reassesses the status of
            %strong negativity
            or conflict-contradiction in logic. The cornerstone of the edifice is the anti-eleatic unity of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/being"} being
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nothing"} nothingness
            in the idea of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} becoming
          %p
            A key passage on Aufhebung in ‘Wissenschaft der Logik’ comes at the end of the first chapter (
            %a{:href => "#WdL"}> I.1.1Cc, p.113
            ): after a deduction of the categories of ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/being"}> being
            ’, ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nothing"}> nothingness
            ’, and their unity in ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"}> becoming
            ’ Hegel determines
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dialectics"} dialectics
            as ‘the higher rational movement… in which the precondition of the separatedness (of the seemingly separated) is lifted (
            %em sich aufhebt
            )’ (
            %a{:href => "#WdL"}> p.111
            ).
          %p
            He goes on (
            %a{:href => "#WdL"}> p.113
            ) to explicate
            %em Aufheben
            as one of the most important concepts in all of philosophy that constantly recurs everywhere. The sublated -
            %em das Aufgehobene
            is not nothing which is an
            = succeed "," do
              %em unmediated
            = succeed ";" do
              %em ein Vermitteltes
            = succeed "," do
              %em das Nichtseiende
            %strong Aufhebung
            is the mode of this coexistence of negation-affirmation.
          %p
            Hegel draws his logic from a rich tradition of dialectic going back to Plato in general and to its renewal in Fichte and Schelling’s attempts to transcendental philosophy in the 1790s for which in turn Kant’s attitude was of capital importance: the latter had established in a short paper
            %em
              ‘
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Attempt+to+Introduce+the+Concept+of+Negative+Quantities+into+Philosophy"}> Versuch den Begriff der negativen Größen in die Weltweisheit einzuführen
              ’
            (1763) a distinction between contradictions and real oppositions
            %sup#fnref:4
              %a{:href => "#fn:4", :rel => "footnote"} 4
            anticipating the later analytic-synthetic division - here originates the term ‘Aufhebung’ and gets tied to synthesis of oppositions which is terminologically present in Schelling’s use of the terms ‘thesis’, ‘anti-thesis’ and ‘synthesis’ for the dialectical triad.
            %sup#fnref:5
              %a{:href => "#fn:5", :rel => "footnote"} 5
          %p
            Secondly, Kant had arranged the table of categories in triadic fashion with the third terms
            %em grosso modo
            being the synthesis of the preceding positive respectively negative terms and claimed to have demonstrated the completeness of the resulting table though his deduction was generally considered inconclusive. Starting with Reinhold the deduction of the categories soon became a main concern of transcendental philosophy. In particular, Fichte rejected the primacy of the judgement forms in Kant’s approach i.e. the primacy of propositions in logic and gave direct derivations of the categories from the transcendental ego by dialectical steps. Schelling and Hegel followed Fichte in this shift.
            %sup#fnref:6
              %a{:href => "#fn:6", :rel => "footnote"} 6
          %p
            Thirdly, Kant’s view that reason gets necessarily entangled in the contradictions of
            %em transcendental dialectic
            by its own nature was interpreted by Hegel as an indication of the positive role of contradictions as the driving force of thought.
          %p Clearly, these remarks can not do justice to the richness and subtlety of Hegel’s logic and should only serve as canvas against which to get a better grasp of Lawvere’s conceptual translation. The points to keep in mind from this view are:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Aufhebung is a
                %em pervasive
                concept: although Lawvere proposes only mathematical definitions for two terms of Hegel’s logic, namely
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Aufhebung
            %li
              %p
                Aufhebung unites
                %em determinateness
                with annihilation of being. These recur at the mathematical level as the correspondence between being-being a sheaf, annihilation -being the adjoint opposite of sheaf (anti-sheaf), and the determination of Aufhebung as being the least (=the Leibnizian best) level of being simultaneously a sheaf and an anti-sheaf.
          %p
            This is the original text on
            %em Aufhebung
            from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#AufhebenDesWerdens"} Hegel 1812, book 1, section 1, chapter 1, C, 3.
          %blockquote
            %p Aufheben und das Aufgehobene (das Ideelle) ist einer der wichtigsten Begriffe der Philosophie, eine Grundbestimmung, die schlechthin allenthalben wiederkehrt, deren Sinn bestimmt aufzufassen und besonders vom Nichts zu unterscheiden ist.—Was sich aufhebt, wird dadurch nicht zu Nichts. Nichts ist das Unmittelbare; ein Aufgehobenes dagegen ist ein Vermitteltes, es ist das Nichtseyende, aber als Resultat, das von einem Seyn ausgegangen ist; es hat daher die Bestimmtheit aus der es herkommt, noch an sich.
            %p Aufheben hat in der Sprache den gedoppelten Sinn, daß es so viel als aufbewahren, erhalten bedeutet, und zugleich so viel als aufhören lassen, ein Ende machen. Das Aufbewahren selbst schließt schon das Negative in sich, daß etwas seiner Unmittelbarkeit und damit einem den äußerlichen Einwirkungen offenen Daseyn entnommen wird, um es zu erhalten.—So ist das Aufgehobene ein zugleich Aufbewahrtes, das nur seine Unmittelbarkeit verloren hat, aber darum nicht vernichtet ist. —Die angegebenen zwei Bestimmungen des Aufhebens können lexikalisch als zwei Bedeutungen dieses Wortes aufgeführt werden. Auffallend müßte es aber dabei seyn, daß eine Sprache dazu gekommen ist, ein und dasselbe Wort für zwei entgegengesetzte Bestimmungen zu gebrauchen. Für das spekulative Denken ist es erfreulich, in der Sprache Wörter zu finden welche eine spekulative Bedeutung an ihnen selbst haben; die deutsche Sprache hat mehrere dergleichen. Der Doppelsinn des lateinischen: tollere (der durch den ciceronianischen Witz tollendum esse Octavium, berühmt geworden) geht nicht so weit, die affirmative Bestimmung geht nur bis zum Emporheben. Etwas ist nur insofern aufgehoben, als es in die Einheit mit seinem Entgegengesetzten getreten ist; in dieser nähern Bestimmung als ein reflektirtes kann es passend Moment genannt werden. Gewicht und Entfernung von einem Punkt heißen beim Hebel, dessen mechanische Momente, um der Dieselbigkeit ihrer Wirkung willen bei aller sonstigen Verschiedenheit eines Reellen, wie das ein Gewicht ist, und eines Ideellen, der bloßen räumlichen Bestimmung, der Linie; s. Encykl. der philos. Wissenschaft 3te Ausg. 261. Anm.—Noch öfter wird die Bemerkung sich aufdringen, daß die philosophische Kunstsprache für reflektirte Bestimmungen lateinische Ausdrücke gebraucht, entweder weil die Muttersprache keine Ausdrücke dafür hat, oder wenn sie deren hat, wie hier, weil ihr Ausdruck mehr an das Unmittelbare, die fremde Sprache aber mehr an das Reflektirte erinnert.
          %h2#lawveres_path_to_aufhebung
            Lawvere’s path to
            %em Aufhebung
          %blockquote
            %p
              In early 1985, while I was studying the foundations of homotopy theory, it occurred to me that the explicit use of a certain simple categorical structure might serve as a link between mathematics and philosophy. (
              %a{:href => "#Law96"}> Lawvere 1996
              , p.167)
          %h2#extracting_the_rational_kernel Extracting the rational kernel
          %p So now let’s get down to business and do some mathematics!
          %h3#the_mathematics_of_yin_and_yang The mathematics of Yin and Yang
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Law00"} Lawvere 2000
            suggested a particularly simple example of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+cylinder"} adjoint cylinder
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            denote the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0, 1,\dots\}
            viewed as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            via their usual
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} ordering
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L,R \colon N\to N
            be the two parallel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            ‘
            %em>
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/even+number"} even
            ’ and ‘
            %em>
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/odd+number"} odd
            ’ defined by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(n) \coloneqq 2n
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(n) \coloneqq 2n+1
          %p
            Both are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} full and faithful
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi even
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{even}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi odd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{odd}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi even
                  %mo ≠
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi odd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{even}\neq N_{odd}
            but are nevertheless ‘identical’ in that there is a bijection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi even
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi odd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{even}\overset{\simeq}{\to} N_{odd}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            whose overall structure they represent in that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi even
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi N
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi odd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{even}\simeq N\simeq N_{odd}
            \- that is somewhat unusual for what is to follow below where the opposing parts are seldom equivalent to the whole but they will always be a pair consisting of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective
            and a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} coreflective subcategory
          %p
            Now it was Lawvere’s observation that a third functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N\to N
            which (with a
            %em clin d’oeil
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Sanders+Peirce"} C. S. Peirce's
            concept of
            = succeed ")" do
              %em thirdness
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\dashv T\dashv R
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The triple expresses the
                %em unity
                by the idempotency of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi T
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mi R
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R\circ T)^2=R\circ T
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi L
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi T
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mi L
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L\circ T)^2=L\circ T
                typical for (co)reflective subcategories,
            %li
              %p
                it expresses the
                %em opposition
                between
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                by an entailed adjunction
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi T
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mi R
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\circ T\dashv R\circ T
            %li
              %p
                it expresses the
                %em identity
                between
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                by the entailed equivalence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi L
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi T
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\circ L\simeq T\circ R
                \.
          %p
            In other words,
            = succeed "!" do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                unites, opposes and identifies
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                at the same time
          %p
            For our simple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            example the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\dashv T
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\dashv R
            amount to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(n)\leq m
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\leq T(m)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\leq R(m)
            iff
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)\leq m
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            exists it must satisfy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi L
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi id
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi T
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\circ L \cong id\cong T\circ R
            which in our case just gives
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                    %mo =
                    %mi T
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\circ L = id = T\circ R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(2n)=n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(2n+1)=n
            which indicates as definition for
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:maxsize => "3em", :minsize => "3em"} {
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac
                            %mi k
                            %mn 2
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mi k
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi N
                            %mi even
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %mi k
                              %mo −
                              %mn 1
                            %mn 2
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mi k
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi N
                            %mi odd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T(k)=\Bigg\{ \array{\frac{k}{2}\quad k\in N_{even} \\
                  \\frac{k-1}{2} \quad k\in N_{odd}}
          %p
            Whereas
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\circ L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\circ R
            are each the identity, the reverse compositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\circ T
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\circ T
            yield an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent comonad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sk
                  %mo :
                  %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sk:N\to N
            and an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent monad
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi cosk
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cosk:N\to N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sk
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sk(2n)=2n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sk
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sk(2n+1)=2n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi cosk
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cosk(2n)=2n+1
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi cosk
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cosk(2n+1)=2n+1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            resurface again but this time within an ‘opposition’
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sk
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi cosk
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sk\dashv cosk
            which expresses formally the ‘conflict’ between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mi even
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{even}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi odd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_{odd}
          %h3#TheMathematicsOfAufhebung
            The mathematics of
            %em Aufhebung
          %p For convenience let us briefly recall the following
          #EssentialLocalization.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization+of+a+category"} localization of a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              whose reflection preserves finite limits. The localization is called
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential
              when the reflection has furthermore a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi r
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l\dashv r\dashv i
            is an essential localization then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l
            is also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} full and faithful
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            is called an
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtopos"} subtopos
            and we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_!\dashv i^*\dashv i_*
            in this case and call
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_!
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %em essentiality
          %p
            It is a result of
            %a{:href => "#KL89"} Kelly &amp; Lawvere 1989
            that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtoposes"} subtoposes
            of a topos form a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete lattice
          #Level.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              An
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essential+geometric+morphism"} essential
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtopos"} subtopos
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              is referred to as a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} level
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              and levels are denoted by small letters
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i,j,\dots
              \.
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_!\dashv i^*\dashv i_*
            yields two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modalities"} adjoint modalities
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mo ◯
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box _i\dashv\bigcirc _i
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo □
                    %mi i
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mo !
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box _i \coloneqq i_!i^*
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mi i
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc _i \coloneqq i_*i^*
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            yield notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"} modal types
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %em i-sheaves
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathcal{B}
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ◯
                        %mi i
                      %mi X
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc _i X\simeq X
                (following the terminology at
                = succeed ");" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+operator"} Lawvere-Tierney operator
            %li
              %p
                the
                %em i-skeleta
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℬ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in\mathcal{B}
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mi i
                      %mi X
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box _i X\simeq X
                (following the example of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+skeleta"} simplicial skeleta
                discussed
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#SimplicialAndCubicalSets"} below
          #Aufhebung.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Law89b"}> Lawvere 1989b
              )
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i,j
              be
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} levels
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Level"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              we say that the level
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is
              %em lower
              than level
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo □
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≺
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo □
                              %mi j
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊥
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊥
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ◯
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≺
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ◯
                              %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Box_i &amp;\prec &amp; \Box_j
                    \\\
                    \\bot &amp;&amp; \bot
                    \\\
                    \\bigcirc_i &amp;\prec &amp; \bigcirc_j
                    }
            %p
              (or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≺
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\prec j
              for short) when every i-sheaf (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_i
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type"}> modal type
              ) is also a j-sheaf and every i-skeleton (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo □
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_i
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type"}> modal type
              ) is a j-skeleton. This is equivalent to say that both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ◯
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc_j \bigcirc_i =\bigcirc_i
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_j \Box_i =\Box_i
            %p
              Let
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\prec j
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              %em resolves the opposite
              of level
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo □
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≪
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo □
                              %mi j
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊥
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⊥
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ◯
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≪
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ◯
                              %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Box_i &amp;\ll&amp; \Box_j
                    \\\
                    \\bot &amp;&amp; \bot
                    \\\
                    \\bigcirc_i &amp;\ll&amp; \bigcirc_j
                    }
            %p
              (or just
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≪
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\ll j
              for short
              %sup#fnref:7>
                %a{:href => "#fn:7", :rel => "footnote"} 7
              ), if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ◯
                        %mi j
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mo □
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc _j\Box_i=\Box _i
            %p
              Finally a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} level
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{i}
              is called the
              %em Aufhebung
              of level
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
