          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  (
                  %a{:href => "#Law89b"}> Lawvere 1989
                  , p.74).
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:2
                %p
                  A pertinent passage is e.g.
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#209"} SoL §209
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:2", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:3
                %p
                  As this polysemy is important for the concept and difficult to preserve in translation we prefer to use the German term in the following.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:3", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:4
                %p
                  On the avatars of this synthetic negation from Kant through von Trendelenburg to Neo-Kantianism see Giovanelli (
                  %a{:href => "#Giovanelli15"}> 2015
                  ). The connection between Kant’s approach to negative quantities and Hegel’s concept of contradiction against the background of the mathematics of their time is developed in the groundbreaking study Wolff (
                  %a{:href => "#Wolff10"}> 2010
                  ) first published in 1981. The importance of this link has been stressed already in the 19th century by
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karl+Rosenkranz"} Karl Rosenkranz
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:4", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:5
                %p
                  Hegel apparently didn’t use these terms though, probably through their use by F. Engels who was a student of Schelling, the terms stick to Hegelian dialectic today.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:5", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:6
                %p
                  Eventually both distanced themselves from the Fichtean ego as the starting point though. Whereas Hegel gives primacy to ‘deduction’ in logic (cf.
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+conclusion"} absolute conclusion
                  \-
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %em ‘Alles Vernünftige ist ein Schluss’
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transcendental+ideal"} transcendental ideal
                  can be partly be seen as a return to the classical Pre-Kantian primacy of ‘concept’ in logic.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:6", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:7
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≪
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ll
                  is called the
                  %em way below
                  relation in (
                  %a{:href => "#KRRZ11"}> KRRZ11
                  ).
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:7", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:8
                %p
                  Lawvere suggests more generally to read
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo □
                          %mi i
                        %mo ⊣
                        %msub
                          %mo ◯
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box_ i\dashv\bigcirc_i
                  as an opposition
                  %em non-F
                  vs.
                  %em pure-F
                  where
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is a property descriptively appropriate for the level.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:8", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
